λx.x:
  Free vars : ∅
  Bound vars: {'x'}
  WHNF?     : True
  Normal form? : True

λx.(x y):
  Free vars : {'y'}
  Bound vars: {'x'}
  WHNF?     : True
  Normal form? : True

(λx.x) z:
  Free vars : {'z'}
  Bound vars: {'x'}
  WHNF?     : False
  Normal form? : False

f ((λx.x) z):
  Free vars : {'f', 'z'}
  Bound vars: {'x'}
  WHNF?     : True
  Normal form? : False

λy.((λx.x) z):
  Free vars : {'z'}
  Bound vars: {'y', 'x'}
  WHNF?     : True
  Normal form? : False

Key distinction:
  Normal Form  - no redexes ANYWHERE in the expression.
  WHNF         - outermost position is not a redex (body may still have them).
  Lazy evaluation (Haskell) only reduces to WHNF: avoids evaluating
  unreachable subexpressions, enabling infinite data structures.
